---
/**
 * Track 11 (Neural Network Intuition), Lesson 3, illustration 2 of 2.
 *
 * The two activation functions side by side. Left: sigmoid, a smooth S-curve
 * that maps the whole number line into (0, 1), passing through 0.5 at x = 0.
 * Right: ReLU, max(0, x), flat at 0 for negative inputs then a 45-degree line
 * for positive inputs (equal x and y scale, so the slope reads as 45 degrees).
 */
// sigmoid panel mappers
const sx = (x: number) => 70 + ((x + 6) / 12) * 270;
const sy = (y: number) => 240 - y * 168;
const sig = (x: number) => 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-x));
let sigPath = '';
for (let x = -6; x <= 6.0001; x += 0.3) sigPath += `${x === -6 ? 'M' : 'L'} ${sx(x).toFixed(1)} ${sy(sig(x)).toFixed(1)} `;

// relu panel mappers (equal scale: 20px per unit on both axes)
const rx = (x: number) => 440 + ((x + 6) / 12) * 240;
const ry = (y: number) => 240 - y * 20;
---

<figure class="cw-illus">
	<svg viewBox="0 0 760 300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-labelledby="nn-sq-title nn-sq-desc">
		<title id="nn-sq-title">Two activation functions: sigmoid and ReLU</title>
		<desc id="nn-sq-desc">Two plots side by side. On the left, the sigmoid function, a smooth S-shaped curve: very negative inputs come out near 0, very positive inputs come out near 1, and an input of 0 comes out at 0.5. On the right, ReLU, the function max of 0 and x: it is flat at 0 for all negative inputs, then rises as a straight 45-degree line for positive inputs, passing the input through unchanged. Both turn the unbounded weighted sum into a usable activation.</desc>

		<!-- ===== sigmoid panel ===== -->
		<text x="205" y="34" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="currentColor">Sigmoid</text>
		<line x1={sx(0)} y1="60" x2={sx(0)} y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.35" />
		<line x1="62" y1={sy(0)} x2="348" y2={sy(0)} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.35" />
		<line x1="70" y1={sy(1)} x2="340" y2={sy(1)} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4" opacity="0.3" />
		<text x="356" y={sy(1) + 4} font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6">1</text>
		<text x="356" y={sy(0) + 4} font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6">0</text>
		<path d={sigPath.trim()} fill="none" stroke="#7c3aed" stroke-width="2.5" />
		<circle cx={sx(0)} cy={sy(0.5)} r="3.5" fill="#7c3aed" />
		<text x={sx(0) + 8} y={sy(0.5) - 6} font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="11" fill="#7c3aed" font-weight="600">0.5</text>
		<text x="205" y="270" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.8">1 / (1 + e^-x)</text>
		<text x="205" y="288" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6">smoothly maps any number into 0 to 1</text>

		<!-- ===== relu panel ===== -->
		<text x="560" y="34" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="currentColor">ReLU</text>
		<line x1={rx(0)} y1="100" x2={rx(0)} y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.35" />
		<line x1="432" y1={ry(0)} x2="692" y2={ry(0)} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.35" />
		<line x1={rx(-6)} y1={ry(0)} x2={rx(0)} y2={ry(0)} stroke="#7c3aed" stroke-width="2.5" />
		<line x1={rx(0)} y1={ry(0)} x2={rx(6)} y2={ry(6)} stroke="#7c3aed" stroke-width="2.5" />
		<circle cx={rx(0)} cy={ry(0)} r="3.5" fill="#7c3aed" />
		<text x={rx(0) - 8} y={ry(0) + 16} text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6">0</text>
		<text x="560" y="270" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.8">max(0, x)</text>
		<text x="560" y="288" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6">negative to 0, positive passes through</text>
	</svg>
	<figcaption>The squish keeps a neuron's output in a usable range. Sigmoid (the traditional pick) eases every number into the open range between 0 and 1. ReLU (common in today's networks) zeroes out negatives and passes positives through unchanged. Which one a network uses is a design choice; both turn the unbounded weighted-sum-plus-bias into a well-behaved activation for the next layer.</figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
	.cw-illus { margin: 1.75rem auto; max-width: 100%; }
	.cw-illus svg { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 720px; height: auto; margin: 0 auto; }
	.cw-illus figcaption { text-align: center; font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.75; margin-top: 0.5rem; font-style: italic; }
</style>
